 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ constant time. The algorithm performs a fixed number of arithmetic operations (subtractions, multiplications, comparisons) regardless of scene complexity [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Only a few scalar or vector variables are needed [S2, unofficial][S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm performs a fixed number of arithmetic operations and comparisons regardless of scene complexity [S2, unofficial][S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Only a few scalar or vector variables are needed [S2, unofficial][S3].
 *   **Performance:** The slabs test is the dominant method for AABB intersection due to its simplicity and efficiency [S2, unofficial][S3]. Branchless implementations are preferred in high-performance ray tracing kernels (e.g., Embree) to maximize throughput on SIMD units and GPUs [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Fast, robust, easy to implement, works well with SIMD/vectorization [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Only applicable to axis-aligned boxes. For oriented bounding boxes (OBBs), a transformation to local space is required, which adds computational cost [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Only applicable to axis-aligned boxes [S2, unofficial][S3].
 
 ## Background
 *   **Origin:** The method was introduced by T. L. Kay and J. T. Kajiya in their 1986 paper "Ray tracing complex scenes" presented at SIGGRAPH [S2, unofficial][S3].
 *   **Development:** The algorithm has become the standard for AABB intersection in ray tracing. It is widely used in modern ray tracing kernels such as Embree and in astronomical simulation tools like raytheia [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Usage:** It is a fundamental building block in volume rendering and ray tracing, used for bounding volume hierarchy (BVH) traversal and primitive intersection tests [S2, unofficial][S3].
+*   **Usage:** It is used for primary ray-scene intersection testing, shadow ray occlusion testing, frustum culling, and spatial traversal of octree and grid acceleration structures [S4].
 
